This file contains more detailed doctests for the stdnum.no.orgnr module. It
tries to cover more corner cases and detailed functionality that is not
really useful as module documentation.

>>> from stdnum.no import orgnr


These have been found online and should all be valid numbers.

>>> numbers = '''
...
... 988 077 917
... 965 920 358
... 980 430 596
... 998 772 680
... 987 008 644
... 917 313 008
... 966 813 946
... 982 930 057
... 982 952 573
... 987 989 297
...
... '''
>>> [x for x in numbers.splitlines() if x and not orgnr.is_valid(x)]
[]


Norwegian organisation numbers always start with 8 or 9. Each of the
following numbers has a valid modulus 11 check digit but an invalid first
digit, so they must all be rejected.

>>> numbers = '''
...
... 000 000 000
... 100 000 008
... 200 000 005
... 300 000 002
... 410 000 008
... 500 000 007
... 600 000 004
... 700 000 001
...
... '''
>>> [x for x in numbers.splitlines() if x and orgnr.is_valid(x)]
[]


Numbers that start with 8 or 9 with a correct check digit remain valid.

>>> orgnr.validate('800 000 009')
'800000009'
>>> orgnr.validate('900 000 006')
'900000006'
